From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fardjad Davari <fardjad.davari2@jumbo.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 23:42:01 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat: add typed OpenRouter EU provider foundation
X-Jcode-Patch-Intent: typed EU deployment identity, auth, caches, and pricing
X-Jcode-Patch-Kind: personal-provider
X-Jcode-Patch-Depends-On: none

Patch intent:
Add OpenRouter EU as a first-class typed deployment and carry stable runtime
identity through authentication, centralized metadata, catalogs, sessions,
remote protocol state, cache selection, endpoint routing, pricing, and canonical
model IDs. This foundation also includes the typed family vocabulary and
integrated enforcement needed to keep the final source state compile-complete.

Why it exists:
OpenRouter EU must use https://eu.openrouter.ai/api/v1, its own API key and env
file, and isolated model and endpoint caches without falling back to standard
OpenRouter credentials or generic prices. A deployment spec replaces identity
inference from display labels, URLs, ambient cache variables, and special
compatible-profile IDs. Provider instances report RuntimeKey, metadata owns one
EU login descriptor and typed auth target, and snapshots carry identity across
remote boundaries. Shared runtime and family foundations are intentionally here
because EU auth, routing, pricing, canonical OpenRouter model IDs, and downstream
policy compile against one identity chain; this patch does not own the documented
default-policy setting.

Upstream integration points:
jcode-provider-core owns OpenRouterDeploymentId, deployment constants,
RuntimeKey, route serialization, provider runtime identity, and shared family
vocabulary. jcode-provider-metadata owns the canonical EU login descriptor and
typed auth target. OpenRouter provider/runtime crates derive endpoint,
credentials, namespace, canonical official model IDs, and pricing from
deployment identity. jcode-base, app-core, protocol, CLI, and TUI preserve identity through
activation, auth, catalogs, switching, sessions, remote events, dispatch, and explicit CLI model selection. Every concrete
runtime reports RuntimeKey instead of relying on labels or execution slots.

Update guidance:
Preserve typed information flow from login metadata to deployment, provider
instance, RuntimeKey, route selection, session, cache, and billing identity.
Never rediscover EU from a display name, API URL, global mode, or profile ID.
Keep EU credentials and both cache classes isolated; pricing must query the EU
namespace before generic fallback. New runtimes must expose stable identity and
new catalog or remote paths must preserve it. Preserve canonicalization of
bare official OpenRouter model names before chat submission. Preserve explicit
CLI model selection for OpenRouter EU without letting generic model-family
inference replace its deployment. Custom compatible profiles continue to receive
their opaque model IDs unchanged. Keep standard OpenRouter, EU, jcode, Grok
Build, and named compatible runtimes distinct even when sharing a slot.

Validation:
./scripts/cargo_exec.sh test -p jcode-provider-core runtime_key_ --lib
./scripts/cargo_exec.sh test -p jcode-provider-openrouter-runtime eu_openrouter --lib
./scripts/cargo_exec.sh test -p jcode-provider-openrouter-runtime openrouter_runtime_normalizes_bare_openai_model_without_provider_pin --lib
./scripts/cargo_exec.sh test -p jcode-provider-openrouter-runtime --lib
./scripts/cargo_exec.sh test -p jcode openrouter_eu_initial_model_spec_preserves_deployment_identity --lib
./scripts/cargo_exec.sh test -p jcode-base openrouter_eu_pricing_uses_its_isolated_catalog_cache --lib
./scripts/cargo_exec.sh test -p jcode-base restricted_policy_blocks_direct_persisted_and_dispatch_bypasses --lib
